--
N5008 [expr.new]/17, /24, [dcl.init.general]/9.3, [dcl.init.aggr]/4-5:
`new int[3]()' and `new int[2]{}' value-initialize (zero) each element,
and `new int[4]{1, 2}' initializes the first two from the list and
value-initializes the others.  The front end value-initialized only
single objects, and a braced list for an array new-expression crashed it
(an invariant violation in typecheck_expr_new, bin53).  The listed
elements are now initialized before the per-element loop
(cpp_new_initializer), which value-initializes the rest.  g++ 13 and
clang++ 18 accept this program, and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
